= The plenum

By early summer the paper has done its work in Moscow, and what it has produced is fear. A General Secretary whose character has been described by the founder of the party cannot carry on as if nothing had been said. Stalin does not deny that the notes exist. He says that a sick man's last words deserve respect, and then he reads out the passages that are hard on his rivals. His apparatus goes to work on us. Emissaries appear in our district committees with polite questions about how a Russian paper found its way to Berlin and who in the Zentrale let it. Moscow's trust in us drops ([? if pl_publish = 1 : sharply, and a promised payment is held back ?][? if pl_publish = 0 : noticeably ?]), and Pieck and Eberlein, the most loyal men in our apparatus, rally to the secretariat.

On 4 July the Central Committee of the Russian party is called together at short notice. Nobody keeps minutes that we are ever shown. We learn what happened from letters, from a delegate's account, and from the faces of the people who come out of the room.

[? if pl_left = 1 : **The Left carries it.** The Opposition had the paper first, and it also has the better argument. Trotsky does not quote Lenin; he lets Lenin be read. Zinoviev and Kamenev, who counted on the troika and have now seen what Lenin thought of them, abstain. It is enough. By a narrow vote the committee decides to do what Lenin asked, and Stalin is removed as General Secretary. The post goes to a collective secretariat that Radek's friends can live with. The troika is finished. Trotsky and Radek take the International in hand, and they have no use for the caution that used to be Zinoviev's. They want the German revolution, and they want it this autumn. ?]

[? if pl_right = 1 : **The Right carries it.** Bukharin, whom everyone likes, and Rykov, who runs the state, stand together, and Tomsky speaks for the unions. They do not ask for Stalin's head, which would hurt the party. They ask that the Secretariat lose the power to appoint the men who vote on it. It is a sober way to end a career, and it works. Stalin is removed as General Secretary and keeps a lesser post. The troika is finished, and Zinoviev and Kamenev are left without a patron. The International passes to Bukharin and Rykov, who have no wish to gamble on a German revolution. Their line is the NEP, the united front and patience. ?]

[? if pl_publish = 1 : **Nobody carries it.** A paper that reached the German newspapers before it reached the Central Committee is not going to be used for anything but a scandal. The oppositions win the argument, but nobody wins a majority. Stalin is not removed. He is put under a control commission and told to answer for his appointments to men who do not trust him. Zinoviev and Kamenev break with him in all but name, and the Russian party is half paralysed for the rest of the year. ?]

[? if pl_left = 1 : In Berlin our Left, which can say it was the first to act, grows much stronger. The Right, whose cautious leaders are now tied to a Moscow that wants a rising, becomes restless. The apparatus men, who had trusted the secretariat, have lost their friend. ?]
[? if pl_right = 1 : In Berlin our Right, which was right about the men who would matter, grows much stronger. The Left, which had hoped for a different victory, becomes restless. The apparatus men have lost their friend in the secretariat. ?]
[? if pl_publish = 1 : In Berlin the Left and the Right both gain ground. Zetkin and the apparatus men, who dislike quarrels and have just been dragged into one, are unhappy. ?]

Where Stalin falls, nobody should take it for a victory of communism over its critics. It is a fight between men who each want a party they can control, and the members are not asked. What the German party has bought with one copy of a letter, and from whom, is a question we will be answering for a long time.

